WEBVTT

00:04.662 --> 00:08.037
Welcome to Dix River Stone, an underground limestone

00:08.037 --> 00:11.612
mine in Garrard County, Kentucky, near Lancaster.

00:11.612 --> 00:17.638
Its MSHA mine ID is 15-18389. The operation was

00:17.638 --> 00:20.925
started in 2001 by the Dix River Stone Company,

00:20.925 --> 00:23.762
and The Allen Company bought it in 2018.

00:23.762 --> 00:26.688
It supplies crushed aggregate to Garrard and surrounding

00:26.688 --> 00:30.175
counties and feeds Allen's Danville Blacktop Plant.

00:30.175 --> 00:32.688
This drawing is only an illustration.

00:32.688 --> 00:34.900
Your instructor will show you the real map.

00:36.012 --> 00:38.737
Dix River works the Camp Nelson Limestone,

00:38.737 --> 00:41.112
the same rock unit mined at Boonesboro.

00:41.112 --> 00:43.825
After Allen bought the site, production was mostly

00:43.825 --> 00:47.200
surface drilling and blasting, until last year.

00:47.200 --> 00:49.650
Surface mining has now stopped. Today,

00:49.650 --> 00:52.062
production is header mining underground on two

00:52.062 --> 00:56.550
levels, Level 1 and Level 2, using room and pillar,

00:56.550 --> 00:59.887
with rooms about 30 by 40 feet. Allen's crew does

00:59.887 --> 01:02.650
the drilling and blasting, and site leadership

01:02.650 --> 01:04.637
includes licensed blasters.

01:05.778 --> 01:08.353
That change matters to you. A site that was mostly

01:08.353 --> 01:11.028
surface work now has the hazards of an underground

01:11.028 --> 01:14.716
mine: ground control at the face, haulage in headings

01:14.716 --> 01:17.641
and escape from two levels. Every shift,

01:17.641 --> 01:20.629
confirm whether you're on Level 1 or Level 2.

01:20.629 --> 01:23.266
Escapeways and maps differ by level.

01:24.384 --> 01:26.834
Shot rock is hauled to the primary crusher on the

01:26.834 --> 01:29.809
surface. From the surge silo it's conveyed to the

01:29.809 --> 01:32.196
secondary plant, where it's crushed,

01:32.196 --> 01:35.334
screened and stockpiled, both on the surface and

01:35.334 --> 01:38.971
underground. Around stockpiles and the surge silo,

01:38.971 --> 01:42.046
bridging and engulfment are serious hazards.

01:42.046 --> 01:44.759
Never walk on a pile or stand under material that

01:44.759 --> 01:47.546
could let go, and follow the site rules for working

01:47.546 --> 01:48.659
near piles.

01:49.795 --> 01:52.107
Here are the focus hazards for Dix River.

01:52.107 --> 01:55.495
Roof and rib conditions on Level 1 and Level 2.

01:55.495 --> 01:58.907
Header mining faces, which need careful examination.

01:58.907 --> 02:01.982
Mobile equipment underground and on the surface.

02:01.982 --> 02:04.595
The primary crusher and the surge silo.

02:04.595 --> 02:06.807
The conveyors to the secondary plant.

02:06.807 --> 02:09.595
Stockpiles on the surface and underground.

02:09.595 --> 02:12.482
Silica dust, noise and diesel equipment.

02:12.482 --> 02:15.595
And emergency escape from both levels to the surface

02:15.595 --> 02:16.957
gathering points.

02:18.084 --> 02:22.221
Underground, carry your W65 self-rescuer and inspect

02:22.221 --> 02:25.221
it as required. You'll do hands-on donning practice

02:25.221 --> 02:27.384
with a training unit, and you should know where

02:27.384 --> 02:30.171
additional units are stored. Around the crusher,

02:30.171 --> 02:33.496
the silo and the plant, respect every guard,

02:33.496 --> 02:35.684
lock out before you work on anything,

02:35.684 --> 02:38.396
and stay out of restricted areas. If you work near

02:38.396 --> 02:41.059
a pond, sump or pump where you could fall into

02:41.059 --> 02:43.684
water, a life jacket is required.

02:47.026 --> 02:50.014
The site supervisor, Gene Smith, has been at this

02:50.014 --> 02:53.076
quarry since it opened in 2001. The foreman is

02:53.076 --> 02:56.289
Braden Walters, and above them, Hunter Kenney is

02:56.289 --> 02:59.264
over all of our sites. Your instructor will introduce

02:59.264 --> 03:02.426
them. Report to them or the designated lead before

03:02.426 --> 03:05.401
starting work. Miners check in and out on the tag

03:05.401 --> 03:08.164
board in the breakroom, next to the time clock,

03:08.164 --> 03:10.576
every time you enter or leave underground,

03:10.576 --> 03:13.739
and never for anyone else. The CB radio and the

03:13.739 --> 03:16.151
scale house are on channel 16.

03:17.284 --> 03:20.284
The speed limit on site is 15 miles an hour.

03:20.284 --> 03:22.709
Haul trucks have the right of way, and all roads

03:22.709 --> 03:25.947
are two-way, right-hand traffic. Radio before you

03:25.947 --> 03:28.384
approach a haul truck, and if you're being loaded,

03:28.384 --> 03:31.034
stay in the cab. There are overhead power lines

03:31.034 --> 03:34.322
on the surface, so no dumping or parking near them,

03:34.322 --> 03:36.522
no raised beds and no tarping.

03:37.642 --> 03:40.554
At Dix River, the conveyors are guarded by railings

03:40.554 --> 03:43.679
along the walkways. Never reach or climb past a

03:43.679 --> 03:46.554
railing while a belt is running, and lock out before

03:46.554 --> 03:49.579
any work on a belt. Hearing protection is required

03:49.579 --> 03:51.767
around the plant while it's running and around

03:51.767 --> 03:54.792
the mine fans. The eyewash bottle is at the scale

03:54.792 --> 03:58.029
house, and the SDS binder is in the breakroom.

03:58.029 --> 04:01.304
Underground, stench gas goes into the intake fan,

04:01.304 --> 04:03.742
with radio and strobe warnings too.

04:03.742 --> 04:07.104
On the surface, the fire warning is a radio call.

04:07.104 --> 04:10.354
Before a blast, there's a siren and strobe beacons,

04:10.354 --> 04:12.692
and entrances to the headers are blocked.

04:12.692 --> 04:15.279
Escapeways are marked on the mine maps with green

04:15.279 --> 04:18.904
arrows. Underground, the primary escapeway is marked

04:18.904 --> 04:21.680
with white reflectors on the ribs, and the secondary

04:21.680 --> 04:24.680
with blue reflectors, along with reflective signs

04:24.680 --> 04:28.055
and arrows. Escape maps are posted in the breakroom

04:28.055 --> 04:30.042
and at the plant trailer on the flat.

04:30.042 --> 04:32.204
The gathering point is the tag board.

04:32.204 --> 04:34.305
Your instructor will walk you through the actual

04:34.305 --> 04:36.992
routes from each level. Mine rescue for all of

04:36.992 --> 04:39.454
our sites comes from the Kentucky Division of Mine

04:39.454 --> 04:42.954
Safety's Crestwood and Hazard mine rescue stations.

04:44.068 --> 04:46.943
On the surface, by the highwall, you'll see two

04:46.943 --> 04:50.381
white steel boxes, each with a red fire extinguisher

04:50.381 --> 04:53.868
stand beside it. Those are the explosive magazines.

04:53.868 --> 04:56.156
Unless you're assigned to handle explosives,

04:56.156 --> 04:58.930
stay away from them, and never smoke or use an

04:58.930 --> 05:02.406
open flame within fifty feet of explosive material.

05:03.526 --> 05:06.264
Underground, you may pass the ANFO trailer,

05:06.264 --> 05:08.526
parked at a heading with cones in front of it.

05:08.526 --> 05:11.376
ANFO is a blasting agent. Give it room,

05:11.376 --> 05:14.039
leave the cones where they are, and the same fifty-foot

05:14.039 --> 05:16.913
rule for smoking and open flames applies.

05:18.040 --> 05:20.565
You'll also see the fuel tank on the surface.

05:20.565 --> 05:22.590
The sign on the end says it plainly:

05:22.590 --> 05:25.953
no open flames. No smoking while you fuel,

05:25.953 --> 05:28.128
and know where the extinguisher on its stand is

05:28.128 --> 05:29.728
before you ever need it.

05:33.063 --> 05:37.163
Since Allen took over in mid-2018, MSHA records

05:37.163 --> 05:40.013
two reportable injuries here, and both were people

05:40.013 --> 05:42.775
falling while stepping down at the end of a shift.

05:42.775 --> 05:45.850
In December 2023, a miner who had just clocked

05:45.850 --> 05:48.375
out fell going down the concrete steps outside

05:48.375 --> 05:51.100
the office and landed on both knees.

05:51.100 --> 05:54.150
In October 2024, a truck driver getting out at

05:54.150 --> 05:56.575
the end of the shift missed the bottom step,

05:56.575 --> 05:59.713
fell and hit the back of his head. Both cost lost

05:59.713 --> 06:02.350
workdays. The shift isn't over until you're in

06:02.350 --> 06:05.738
your vehicle. Use the handrails, face the cab,

06:05.738 --> 06:08.175
and keep three points of contact all the way to

06:08.175 --> 06:10.688
the ground, especially when you're tired.

06:11.817 --> 06:14.079
Both of those injuries happened while production

06:14.079 --> 06:17.467
was mostly surface work. Now Dix River mines headers

06:17.467 --> 06:20.254
underground in the same rock worked at Boonesboro,

06:20.254 --> 06:23.229
where falling face rock, haulage and scaling have

06:23.229 --> 06:25.992
hurt miners. Treat ground control here with the

06:25.992 --> 06:28.629
same care those older underground mines have learned

06:28.629 --> 06:31.717
to use. Work only under examined ground,

06:31.717 --> 06:35.267
report cracking, spalling, water or unusual sounds

06:35.267 --> 06:37.842
right away, and scale only if you're trained and

06:37.842 --> 06:38.942
authorized.

06:40.070 --> 06:44.645
Inspectors issued 24 citations at Dix River from 2021

06:44.645 --> 06:48.795
through mid-October 2026. The most cited were electrical,

06:48.795 --> 06:51.570
six times for circuit protection, controls,

06:51.570 --> 06:54.508
conductors and dangerous conditions not corrected,

06:54.508 --> 06:57.895
and equipment defects, five times. Report damaged

06:57.895 --> 07:01.633
cords, cables, boxes and switches, and never repair

07:01.633 --> 07:04.183
electrical equipment unless you're qualified.

07:04.183 --> 07:07.058
Do your Quarry Equipment Pre-Operational Inspection

07:07.058 --> 07:09.745
every shift, and turn it in at the scale house

07:09.745 --> 07:12.195
or on the app. The record's takeaway:

07:12.195 --> 07:15.120
pre-shift checks, electrical condition and stepping

07:15.120 --> 07:18.120
down safely. And as header mining grows,

07:18.120 --> 07:20.908
ground control and haulage are the hazards to watch

07:20.908 --> 07:22.120
most closely.

07:23.253 --> 07:25.990
Before your first shift underground at Dix River,

07:25.990 --> 07:28.103
make sure you can answer four questions.

07:28.103 --> 07:31.478
Am I on Level 1 or Level 2 today? What are my primary

07:31.478 --> 07:33.603
and secondary escapeways from there?

07:33.603 --> 07:35.978
Where is my gathering point? And who do I report

07:35.978 --> 07:38.990
to, and how do I reach them on channel 16?

07:38.990 --> 07:41.478
If you can't answer all four, ask before you go

07:41.478 --> 07:44.778
in. Dix River is changing fast, and asking is how

07:44.778 --> 07:45.790
you keep up.

07:46.922 --> 07:50.085
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

07:50.085 --> 07:51.585
override this video.
